1
00:00:01,040 --> 00:00:02,120
Hello, English learners.

2
00:00:02,520 --> 00:00:03,920
Welcome back to EnglishPod.

3
00:00:03,920 --> 00:00:04,420
My name is Marco.

4
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:05,380
And I'm Erica.

5
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:09,060
And today we're bringing you another great lesson about appointments.

6
00:00:09,920 --> 00:00:10,920
That's right.

7
00:00:10,920 --> 00:00:13,980
We've got an elementary lesson about cancelling an appointment.

8
00:00:15,120 --> 00:00:16,800
It's a really common situation sometimes.

9
00:00:17,460 --> 00:00:19,660
You have an appointment at the doctor's or something.

10
00:00:19,920 --> 00:00:20,220
Or at work.

11
00:00:21,160 --> 00:00:21,980
And you have to cancel it.

12
00:00:22,160 --> 00:00:23,160
Yes.

13
00:00:23,160 --> 00:00:26,260
So we're going to learn some great language to help you cancel appointments today.

14
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:28,440
Let's go straight to the dialogue then.

15
00:00:28,800 --> 00:00:32,580
We're going to be listening as Samantha and Angela have a phone conversation.

16
00:00:32,580 --> 00:00:35,640
And Angela is trying to cancel an appointment.

17
00:00:36,160 --> 00:00:37,920
And it's going to be a little bit fast in the beginning.

18
00:00:37,920 --> 00:00:41,320
But when we come back, we will explain all the great vocabulary.

19
00:00:42,640 --> 00:00:46,640
Hello, Samantha speaking.

20
00:00:46,640 --> 00:00:49,640
Hi, Samantha.

21
00:00:49,640 --> 00:00:55,640
This is Angela calling.

22
00:00:55,640 --> 00:00:58,640
Oh, hi, Angela.

23
00:00:58,760 --> 00:00:58,960
What's up?

24
00:00:59,370 --> 00:01:01,250
I'm just calling about our meeting today.

25
00:01:02,090 --> 00:01:05,690
I wonder, is it possible to reschedule our appointment in the afternoon?

26
00:01:06,250 --> 00:01:09,090
I have a bit of an emergency that I need to take care of.

27
00:01:09,720 --> 00:01:10,440
Let me see.

28
00:01:10,640 --> 00:01:12,640
It shouldn't be too much of a problem.

29
00:01:12,690 --> 00:01:13,550
I'm really sorry.

30
00:01:14,270 --> 00:01:16,430
I hope it doesn't inconvenience you too much.

31
00:01:16,970 --> 00:01:19,070
It's just this thing came up and...

32
00:01:19,070 --> 00:01:20,010
Angela, you know what?

33
00:01:20,250 --> 00:01:21,430
I can't make it to our meeting either.

34
00:01:21,970 --> 00:01:24,510
Why don't we postpone it to tomorrow afternoon at the same time?

35
00:01:24,870 --> 00:01:25,470
Sounds great.

36
00:01:25,770 --> 00:01:26,290
See you tomorrow.

37
00:01:30,340 --> 00:01:31,220
Angela, look up.

38
00:01:31,640 --> 00:01:34,640
See that lady over there who's trying on a red leather jacket?

39
00:01:34,640 --> 00:01:34,980
Isn't that Samantha?

40
00:01:35,800 --> 00:01:35,940
What?

41
00:01:36,780 --> 00:01:39,260
No wonder she told me she couldn't make it to the meeting.

42
00:01:39,920 --> 00:01:40,220
Oh, no.

43
00:01:40,420 --> 00:01:41,500
I think she saw me.

44
00:01:46,500 --> 00:01:50,300
Okay, we have some really interesting words that you probably listened to in this dialogue.

45
00:01:50,300 --> 00:01:52,740
So let's start with language takeaway.

46
00:01:56,180 --> 00:01:58,640
Today on language takeaway, we're bringing you three words.

47
00:01:59,100 --> 00:01:59,640
Erica, what's the first one?

48
00:02:00,380 --> 00:02:02,640
The first word is reschedule.

49
00:02:02,640 --> 00:02:03,640
Reschedule.

50
00:02:03,640 --> 00:02:04,120
Reschedule.

51
00:02:04,640 --> 00:02:05,020
Reschedule.

52
00:02:07,060 --> 00:02:09,560
When you reschedule an appointment, you...

53
00:02:09,560 --> 00:02:11,940
Change it for another day or time.

54
00:02:12,180 --> 00:02:12,420
Exactly.

55
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:13,480
Great.

56
00:02:13,780 --> 00:02:15,640
The second word that we have is inconvenience.

57
00:02:15,640 --> 00:02:15,840
Inconvenience.

58
00:02:17,640 --> 00:02:18,640
Inconvenience.

59
00:02:18,640 --> 00:02:20,640
Inconvenience.

60
00:02:20,640 --> 00:02:23,640
So, to inconvenience someone is to...

61
00:02:23,920 --> 00:02:26,640
To make things not convenient for that person.

62
00:02:26,640 --> 00:02:27,640
Right.

63
00:02:27,640 --> 00:02:30,640
So, to make things a little bit difficult or to make things a little bit...

64
00:02:30,890 --> 00:02:31,450
Uncomfortable.

65
00:02:31,510 --> 00:02:32,030
Uncomfortable.

66
00:02:33,810 --> 00:02:34,330
Inconvenience.

67
00:02:34,750 --> 00:02:36,950
And our third word is...

68
00:02:36,950 --> 00:02:37,550
Postpone.

69
00:02:37,640 --> 00:02:38,640
Postpone.

70
00:02:38,640 --> 00:02:38,970
Postpone.

71
00:02:39,750 --> 00:02:40,270
Postpone.

72
00:02:41,250 --> 00:02:46,490
We have some examples of the word postpone that will help you to understand its meaning a little bit better.

73
00:02:46,790 --> 00:02:47,170
Let's listen.

74
00:02:47,640 --> 00:02:50,640
Example 1.

75
00:02:51,350 --> 00:02:53,970
Since Robert is late again, we have to postpone the meeting.

76
00:02:57,640 --> 00:03:01,640
Example 2.

77
00:03:02,380 --> 00:03:05,400
I'm sorry, Simon, but your vacation has been postponed until next month.

78
00:03:08,640 --> 00:03:12,640
Example 3.

79
00:03:12,640 --> 00:03:14,480
Unfortunately, the flight will be postponed.

80
00:03:17,610 --> 00:03:20,190
Okay, so basically postpone is to delay.

81
00:03:20,650 --> 00:03:21,030
That's right.

82
00:03:21,490 --> 00:03:22,670
Make it at a later time.

83
00:03:22,790 --> 00:03:23,490
At a later time.

84
00:03:24,330 --> 00:03:29,690
Okay, some great words there and we can listen to our dialogue a second time at a slower speed.

85
00:03:38,860 --> 00:03:40,440
Hello, Samantha speaking.

86
00:03:41,230 --> 00:03:41,970
Hi, Samantha.

87
00:03:42,910 --> 00:03:44,030
This is Angela calling.

88
00:03:44,730 --> 00:03:45,850
Oh, hi, Angela.

89
00:03:46,350 --> 00:03:46,690
What's up?

90
00:03:47,350 --> 00:03:49,690
I'm just calling about our meeting today.

91
00:03:50,570 --> 00:03:55,350
I wonder, is it possible to reschedule our appointment in the afternoon?

92
00:03:56,050 --> 00:04:00,070
I have a bit of an emergency that I need to take care of.

93
00:04:00,810 --> 00:04:01,750
Let me see.

94
00:04:02,370 --> 00:04:04,490
It shouldn't be too much of a problem.

95
00:04:05,250 --> 00:04:06,330
I'm really sorry.

96
00:04:07,050 --> 00:04:09,910
I hope it doesn't inconvenience you too much.

97
00:04:10,690 --> 00:04:13,330
It's just this thing came up and…

98
00:04:13,640 --> 00:04:15,050
Angela, you know what?

99
00:04:15,640 --> 00:04:17,350
I can't make it to our meeting either.

100
00:04:17,910 --> 00:04:21,690
Why don't we postpone it to tomorrow afternoon at the same time?

101
00:04:22,230 --> 00:04:23,130
Sounds great.

102
00:04:24,030 --> 00:04:24,610
See you tomorrow.

103
00:04:26,530 --> 00:04:28,890
Angela, Angela, look up.

104
00:04:29,760 --> 00:04:32,840
See that lady over there who's trying on a red leather jacket?

105
00:04:33,660 --> 00:04:34,420
Isn't that Samantha?

106
00:04:35,380 --> 00:04:36,000
What?

107
00:04:36,980 --> 00:04:40,420
No wonder she told me she couldn't make it to the meeting.

108
00:04:41,420 --> 00:04:41,640
Oh, no.

109
00:04:42,520 --> 00:04:44,100
I think she saw me.

110
00:04:50,580 --> 00:04:55,220
Alright, so in this dialogue, we had some really great phrases that we should look at in Fluency Builder.

111
00:04:57,800 --> 00:05:02,420
We have three phrases for you today in Fluency Builder, and the first one is so common.

112
00:05:02,420 --> 00:05:03,520
It's a great phrase.

113
00:05:04,160 --> 00:05:04,740
What's up?

114
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:10,500
So, this is a very common way of saying hi.

115
00:05:11,020 --> 00:05:11,160
Yeah.

116
00:05:11,540 --> 00:05:11,740
What's up?

117
00:05:12,140 --> 00:05:12,620
How's it going?

118
00:05:12,920 --> 00:05:13,460
What's going on?

119
00:05:13,840 --> 00:05:18,020
Right, and now the interesting thing is that many people don't know how to answer this phrase.

120
00:05:18,380 --> 00:05:20,360
Yes, I see this all the time with my students.

121
00:05:20,540 --> 00:05:22,700
So, if you say what's up, you should say…

122
00:05:23,240 --> 00:05:23,600
Not much.

123
00:05:23,640 --> 00:05:24,640
Not much.

124
00:05:24,640 --> 00:05:25,060
Nothing.

125
00:05:25,640 --> 00:05:26,640
Nothing.

126
00:05:26,640 --> 00:05:27,640
So, what's up?

127
00:05:27,640 --> 00:05:28,640
Not much.

128
00:05:28,640 --> 00:05:29,640
Not much.

129
00:05:29,640 --> 00:05:32,640
Alright, so the next time someone tells you what's up, now you know what to answer.

130
00:05:32,640 --> 00:05:33,640
Yeah.

131
00:05:33,640 --> 00:05:36,280
Okay, our second phrase is can't make it.

132
00:05:36,660 --> 00:05:37,780
Can't make it.

133
00:05:38,160 --> 00:05:39,240
I can't make it.

134
00:05:39,540 --> 00:05:42,460
We use this phrase when we want to say I cannot attend.

135
00:05:42,840 --> 00:05:43,720
I cannot make it.

136
00:05:43,820 --> 00:05:44,640
Yeah, I can't come.

137
00:05:45,300 --> 00:05:49,220
Right, so if you're having a party today, I say, oh, I'm sorry, I can't make it.

138
00:05:49,640 --> 00:05:50,940
It means I can't go.

139
00:05:51,640 --> 00:05:52,640
Exactly.

140
00:05:52,640 --> 00:05:53,640
Maybe one more example.

141
00:05:53,640 --> 00:05:55,640
So, Marco, we've got a meeting today at three o'clock.

142
00:05:56,250 --> 00:05:57,130
I can't make it.

143
00:05:57,370 --> 00:05:58,130
I'm recording today.

144
00:05:58,350 --> 00:05:58,990
Oh, okay.

145
00:05:59,790 --> 00:06:01,190
So, can't make it.

146
00:06:01,640 --> 00:06:05,640
Our last word for fluency builder, this thing came up.

147
00:06:05,640 --> 00:06:06,850
This thing came up.

148
00:06:07,640 --> 00:06:08,790
This thing came up.

149
00:06:09,640 --> 00:06:13,640
This is a great way of giving an excuse for why you can't make it to an event.

150
00:06:13,640 --> 00:06:14,640
Right.

151
00:06:14,640 --> 00:06:17,450
So, I can't make it today because this thing came up.

152
00:06:17,640 --> 00:06:18,640
Like, something…

153
00:06:18,770 --> 00:06:19,370
Something happened.

154
00:06:19,640 --> 00:06:20,640
Something happened.

155
00:06:20,640 --> 00:06:21,640
Yeah.

156
00:06:21,640 --> 00:06:25,090
Let's listen to a few examples of this phrase so we can see how it's used in the wild.

157
00:06:25,640 --> 00:06:27,640
Example one.

158
00:06:27,640 --> 00:06:34,640
I'm sorry that I'm late, this thing came up at work and I had to stay to take care of it.

159
00:06:35,680 --> 00:06:36,440
Example two.

160
00:06:38,640 --> 00:06:39,640
I can't talk right now.

161
00:06:40,020 --> 00:06:40,580
Something came up.

162
00:06:43,640 --> 00:06:46,640
Example three.

163
00:06:47,150 --> 00:06:48,170
Sorry I missed the meeting.

164
00:06:48,170 --> 00:06:49,090
Something came up.

165
00:06:51,830 --> 00:06:54,870
Okay, great example and I guess it's perfectly clear now.

166
00:06:55,030 --> 00:06:55,330
Yes.

167
00:06:55,790 --> 00:06:59,750
So, let's listen to our dialogue a third time and now we're ready to understand everything.

168
00:07:27,330 --> 00:07:28,430
I'm really sorry.

169
00:07:29,050 --> 00:07:31,250
I hope it doesn't inconvenience you too much.

170
00:07:31,830 --> 00:07:33,830
It's just this thing came up and…

171
00:07:39,710 --> 00:07:40,270
Sounds great.

172
00:08:01,800 --> 00:08:06,040
So, Erica, have you ever cancelled an appointment like this under false pretenses?

173
00:08:06,820 --> 00:08:09,380
So, for a fake reason?

174
00:08:09,640 --> 00:08:11,500
For a fake reason to go shopping?

175
00:08:13,300 --> 00:08:20,140
I may not have told my boss so I can't make it to a meeting because this thing came up and then I really was going shopping.

176
00:08:20,640 --> 00:08:21,540
I've never done that.

177
00:08:21,740 --> 00:08:22,480
But how about you, Marco?

178
00:08:23,100 --> 00:08:25,400
Well, you know, in Ecuador, soccer is a really big thing.

179
00:08:25,680 --> 00:08:25,800
Right.

180
00:08:26,640 --> 00:08:29,640
So, when we were playing in the World Cup, Ecuador was playing and…

181
00:08:30,400 --> 00:08:31,220
Not you, but…

182
00:08:31,220 --> 00:08:34,080
Well, no, like the Ecuadorian soccer team.

183
00:08:34,640 --> 00:08:37,640
I cancelled an appointment because I was going to go watch the game.

184
00:08:38,120 --> 00:08:38,260
Really?

185
00:08:38,640 --> 00:08:38,940
A business appointment.

186
00:08:39,640 --> 00:08:39,980
A business appointment.

187
00:08:40,640 --> 00:08:41,420
Well, I guess that's okay.

188
00:08:42,040 --> 00:08:50,240
Yeah, I mean, it was like the first time we were ever in the World Cup and, you know, my boss didn't really put up much of a fight because I guess he also wanted to go watch the game.

189
00:08:50,420 --> 00:08:53,120
So, very similar to the events in this dialogue.

190
